																		I&#039;d Rather Be Part of the Serpent&#039;s Hand Than the Foundation&quot;
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/joshua-aaron" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5612221); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5612221&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607485206" alt="Joshua Aaron" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5612221)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/joshua-aaron" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5612221); return false;" >Joshua Aaron</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607101658 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">04 Dec 2020 17:07</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4836222">
									

<p>So, I think the Serpent's Hand would be better to be a part of / work for than the SCP Foundation. Here are all my points, in no particular order:</p>
<p>1. Foundation operates out of boringly-decorated, overly sanitary-looking research facilities filled with deadly monsters that can get loose at any second, not to mention they are constantly raided by the Chaos Insurgency. The Serpent's Hand gets a frikin' extradimensional library that requires actual frikin' magic to access, and is actually very safe because all of the anomalies within are friendly as long as nobody gets violent against them or breaks the rules. Nice place to chill and relax, maybe play some Dungeons and Dragons and write in peace.</p>
<p>2. Foundation deprives anomalous humans of their humanity, and instead locks them up in basically prison (I don't give a crap about how much you <em>think</em> Iris Thompson likes it there, she most likely secretly hates being locked up, along with literally any other human SCP).</p>
<p>3. They fight for the rights of law-abiding anomalous humans that just want to go about their lives without having to worry about being either imprisonated by the SCP Foundation, murdered by the Global Occult Coalition, weaponized by the Chaos Insurgency, or sold by MC&amp;D. Basically, they tell everybody else to chill their heels and leave anomalous people the heck alone. Which is actually a pretty noble cause. The only reason they raid Foundation / GoC locations is because that is one of the only ways to get their points across. Plus, they do stealth missions, and kill Foundation personnel in only a limited capacity.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>4. The Serpent's Hand doesn't breach dangerous anomalies for no good reason at all. That's the Chaos Insurgency's job, after all. If the Serpent's Hand doesn't have a good reason to breach any given SCP during a raid, they won't breach said SCP. They do, however, steal Safe SCP items, and let peaceful human SCPs to escape. They may even attempt to free D-class if possible. They're not stupid, and they don't want to bring about the end of the world by releasing highly dangerous Euclid, Keter, and Apollyon SCPs. Again, that is the Chaos Insurgency's job.</p>
<p>5. Simply existing as a worker on a Foundation site means that you are alive on moment, and then the next you are dead due to being freakin' eaten by a massive lizard that is literally too angry to die or turned into a zombie by a dude cosplaying as a medieval plague doctor, and your friends and family will be unaware of your death (if all of them are outside of the foundation, which is most likely). If you do manage to survive through the containment breach, you will still be mentally scarred by the experience. As I said earlier, the Serpent's Hand operates out of a peaceful library, with a 0% chance of being eaten by a bloodthirsty monster.</p>
<p>6. I also believe that the serpent's hand probably is one of the morally better GoIs, considering their entire mission statement is &quot;we want rights for anomalous humans, and knowledge about the paranormal should be public.&quot; While they do break the law from time to time (which would make them Chaotic or Neutral Good), they don't do so for no reason at all. They just want the world to be a better place.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
